{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-35509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-35509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-35509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a6164dbd78847269eb014c3525ee5d59e57d2201d213db80413e1f39e31720b"} {"question" "q-35509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-35509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,0,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(-27,0,0)$ and $(27,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-3\\sqrt{3},0,0)$ and $(3\\sqrt{3},0,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(2,0,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-35509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the sphere $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ 16+{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a6164dbd78847269eb014c3525ee5d59e57d2201d213db80413e1f39e31720b"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=41"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=9"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=36"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-71947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a6164dbd78847269eb014c3525ee5d59e57d2201d213db80413e1f39e31720b"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,1,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=5,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}) \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(2,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a6164dbd78847269eb014c3525ee5d59e57d2201d213db80413e1f39e31720b"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $5$  centered at  $(-2,-2)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $5$  centered at  $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$  centered at  $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$  centered at  $(2,-2)$"] ["text" "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$  centered at  $(2,2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$  centered at  $(2,-2)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the ellipsoid $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a6164dbd78847269eb014c3525ee5d59e57d2201d213db80413e1f39e31720b"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{3}}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}\\le 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069828/state.json" "file_sha256" "eed4c28e06689de4286057104f0938c9c44eec73a577d70a7a773ebe699dcd65"} {"question" "q-164408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [: